(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ophthalmology-i-vignettes"]={"title":"Common Ophthalmological Disorders Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis has severe deep eye pain that wakes her at night and radiates to her face. There is a bluish-purple patch on the white of the eye and the vessels will not move with a cotton-tip applicator. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Scleritis","Episcleritis","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Viral conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — night pain, violaceous hue and immobile vessels, on an autoimmune background. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man is considering laser ablation for xanthelasma. What should he be told about the outcome?","choices":["Even after effective local treatment, recurrences are common","Local treatment is curative and recurrence is rare","The lesions will resolve on their own if left alone","The lesions will become malignant if not removed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck flags recurrence explicitly. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man complains that his upper lids feel heavy and he is looking through his lashes. There are excess folds of skin over both upper lids. Which assessment most directly determines whether surgery will be covered?","choices":["Slit lamp examination with fluorescein","Computed tomography of the orbits","Visual field testing","Intraocular pressure measurement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a demonstrated field deficit is what the deck ties to insurance coverage for blepharoplasty. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with a painful red eye is found on slit lamp to have white deposits on the back surface of the cornea. What are these called?","choices":["Follicles","Keratic precipitates","Drusen","Hard exudates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — white blood cell deposits on the corneal endothelium. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has a painless bright red patch on the white of one eye with no obvious precipitant and no medication history to explain it. Vision, pupil and cornea are all normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Take a careful history and check his blood pressure","Refer urgently to ophthalmology","Start a topical antibiotic","Order computed tomography of both orbits"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck calls history super important and names the blood pressure check when there is no explanation. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with confirmed non-infectious anterior uveitis is under ophthalmology care. Which treatment does the lecture describe as typical?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids","Intraocular corticosteroid injection","Oral antivirals for ten days","Topical fluoroquinolone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — for non-infectious anterior disease the deck gives topical steroids. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman from a trachoma-endemic region has eyelashes turned inward and abrading her cornea after years of repeated infection. Which is required?","choices":["A further single dose of azithromycin","Lubricating drops alone","Observation with annual review","Surgical treatment of the trichiasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck states that trichiasis requires surgery; antibiotics cannot reverse scarring. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has pain, redness and swelling over the outer third of her upper eyelid, with an enlarged node in front of the ear on the same side. Workup points to an inflammatory cause. Which treatment does the lecture give, and how quickly should it work?","choices":["Intravenous antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours","Oral corticosteroids, with a response expected within 48 hours","Oral antibiotics, with a response expected within 48 hours","Cool compresses alone, with resolution over two weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck's agent and response window for inflammatory dacryoadenitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A healthy 28-year-old man has three days of a red right eye with thick yellow discharge and lids stuck together in the morning. Vision is normal, the cornea is clear, and there is no preauricular node. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Oral doxycycline twice daily for seven days","A topical histamine blocker with a mast cell stabiliser","Oral aciclovir for ten days","A topical broad-spectrum antibiotic such as a fluoroquinolone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an immunocompetent adult with uncomplicated bacterial conjunctivitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has a swollen red eyelid, fever, and pain on moving the eye. The globe is proptotic and she has double vision. Which investigation is most appropriate?","choices":["Fluorescein angiography","Computed tomography of the orbits and paranasal sinuses with contrast","Slit lamp examination with fluorescein alone","Conjunctival nucleic acid amplification testing for chlamydia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — post-septal features mean orbital involvement must be imaged. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has had three separate episodes of anterior uveitis in two years and reports joint pain and lower back stiffness. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure him that recurrence is expected and needs no further workup","Start long-term prophylactic topical antibiotics","Arrange fluorescein angiography","Thorough systemic evaluation for an underlying autoimmune condition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck calls for this when uveitis is recurrent or has suggestive systemic features. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man developed a tender red lump at his eyelid margin overnight. Two weeks of warm compresses have not helped and it persists. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer to ophthalmology for incision and drainage","Continue compresses for another three months","Start oral corticosteroids","Perform incision and drainage in the clinic yourself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a hordeolum that has not improved in two weeks meets the deck's referral threshold. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man is starting warm compresses for a chalazion. What should he be told about the timescale?","choices":["It will not improve without surgery","Improvement may take months","It should be gone within 48 hours","It should be gone within one week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck says so, and setting this expectation prevents a premature return. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with a painful dermatomal rash across one side of his forehead has corneal lesions on fluorescein that branch but lack elevated edges and have no knobs at the branch tips. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acanthamoeba keratitis","Bacterial keratitis","Herpes zoster keratitis","Herpes simplex keratitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pseudodendrites lack the elevated edges and terminal end bulbs of a true dendrite. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has had viral conjunctivitis for four weeks and now reports significant photophobia and blurred vision. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure and continue cool compresses","Start a topical antibiotic","Start a topical corticosteroid","Refer to ophthalmology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — beyond three weeks, or with photophobia or decreased vision after onset, the deck refers. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with itchy bilateral red eyes has bumps on the inner lid surface that are red at the surface and paler at the base. What do these indicate?","choices":["Papillae, which point to a bacterial or allergic cause","Follicles, which point to a chlamydial or viral cause","Keratic precipitates, which point to anterior uveitis","Hard exudates, which point to diabetic retinopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — papillae are red at the surface and paler at the base. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with a chronically red eye has pale bumps on the inner surface of the lower lid that look redder at their base. What do these indicate?","choices":["Keratic precipitates, which point to anterior uveitis","Drusen, which point to macular degeneration","Follicles, which point to a chlamydial or viral cause","Papillae, which point to a bacterial or allergic cause"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — follicles are pale at the surface and redder at the base. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with rosacea has months of burning, gritty eyes with crusting and scaling at the lash bases and thickened, toothpaste-like secretion from the lid margins. Which is the most appropriate first-line management?","choices":["Topical antibiotic ointment","Oral antibiotics","Urgent ophthalmology referral","Lid hygiene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck's first step, escalating only if two weeks of it fails. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has soft yellowish oval plaques on the inner aspect of both upper eyelids. They are asymptomatic. Which workup is most appropriate?","choices":["Serum lipid profile alone","Complete blood count with differential, plus blood cultures","No workup — the lesions are purely cosmetic","Serum lipid profile, together with tests for diabetes mellitus and liver function"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck treats these plaques as a marker of an underlying metabolic disorder. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has been diagnosed with chlamydial conjunctivitis confirmed on conjunctival testing. Beyond treating her eye, which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer her urgently to ophthalmology","Screen her for autoimmune disease","Arrange evaluation for other sexually transmitted infections and notify her partners for evaluation","Repeat the conjunctival test in one week to confirm that it has cleared"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the eye finding is a sentinel for a usually asymptomatic urogenital infection. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman developed a painful, tender, red lump at the margin of her lower eyelid overnight. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Xanthelasma","Hordeolum","Chalazion","Dacryocystitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — acute onset over 24 hours, tender, and at the lid margin. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has a painful vesicular rash over his forehead and one upper eyelid that stops sharply at the midline, and a vesicle on the tip of his nose. What does the nasal-tip lesion indicate?","choices":["That the rash will cross the midline within days","That the infection is herpes simplex rather than herpes zoster","That the patient is immunocompromised","Nasociliary branch involvement, and therefore a higher risk of ocular involvement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Hutchinson sign, and its significance is the ocular risk. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman reports a constant foreign body sensation in one eye. The lower lid margin turns inward and the lashes contact the globe. Which examination is most appropriate?","choices":["Fluorescein angiography","Serum lipid profile","Slit lamp examination to assess whether the cornea has been involved","Computed tomography of the orbits and sinuses with contrast"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the question with entropion is always whether the lashes have damaged the cornea. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with Sjögren disease has recurrent diffuse redness of both eyes with minimal discomfort and no discharge, alongside malaise and fatigue. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["A course of topical antibiotics","Oral doxycycline for seven days","Routine referral to ophthalmology","Emergent referral the same day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck manages autoimmune conjunctivitis with routine referral. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has a moderate bacterial corneal ulcer that sits directly in front of his pupil. How does its position affect the outlook?","choices":["The prognosis is worse, because ulcers inside the visual axis carry a poorer outcome","Position makes no difference; only ulcer size matters","The prognosis is better, because central ulcers are noticed and treated sooner","The prognosis depends only on the organism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck grades prognosis by both size and position relative to the visual axis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man who grew up in a region with poor sanitation has inturned eyelashes rubbing his cornea, with scarring of the upper eyelid and a history of repeated childhood eye infections. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial conjunctivitis","Trachoma","Adult inclusion conjunctivitis","Blepharitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — repeated chlamydial infection scars the lid, causing entropion and then trichiasis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a firm, painless lump in her upper eyelid that has been slowly enlarging for three weeks. It is not tender. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Oral antibiotics for ten days","Urgent referral to exclude sebaceous carcinoma","Warm compresses with gentle massage","Incision and drainage today"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the painless, slowly enlarging nodule is a chalazion, managed conservatively first. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A public health team is treating an area where trachoma prevalence is eight per cent. Which regimen does the lecture describe?","choices":["Azithromycin one gram weekly for four weeks","Azithromycin one gram orally as a single dose, given to all eligible members of the evaluation unit","Doxycycline 100 milligrams twice daily for seven days, to symptomatic individuals only","Topical erythromycin ointment to symptomatic individuals only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — mass drug administration, at or above the five per cent threshold. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has a swollen red eyelid, fever, and pain on eye movement. The eye is pushed forward, movements are restricted, and she reports double vision and blurring. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Post-septal (orbital) cellulitis","Pre-septal (periorbital) cellulitis","Anterior uveitis","Acute allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — proptosis, painful restricted movement and reduced vision are all post-septal features. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has a tender, tense, erythematous swelling over the nasal aspect of her lower eyelid, below the medial canthal tendon, with mucoid material expressible from the lower punctum. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chalazion","Dacryocystitis","A lacrimal sac tumour","Dacryoadenitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the location below the tendon and the expressible discharge are both characteristic. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"}]};
